── linux ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #137  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Clarifies that device measurements must be live-fetched (not cached) and that only the complete transcript ending with the signed final MEASUREMENTS is authoritative; trusting stale or partial measurements enables attestation bypass.
  [BUG] drivers/pci/tsm.c:618-626  authenticated_show
      The 'authenticated' sysfs attribute is unconditionally identity-mapped to the 'connect' state. The code comment at line 622-624 explicitly documents this design decision: 'When the SPDM session established via TSM the 'authenticated' state of the device is identical to the connect state.' No cryptographic re-verification is performed — no SPDM CHALLENGE, no GET_MEASUREMENTS re-fetch, no transcript validation. A VMM that maintains the pdev->tsm pointer while allowing the underlying SPDM session to lapse or be re-established with a compromised device will cause this node to return a false positive. This directly matches the vulnerability pattern: trusting stale/cached session state as a proxy for cryptographic proof of device authentication.
  [RISK] drivers/pci/tsm.c:230-246  connect_show
      connect_show reads pdev->tsm under pci_tsm_rwsem (read lock) and reports the TSM device name. The pdev->tsm pointer is used as a binary 'connected/not-connected' indicator — if non-NULL, the device is reported as connected. However, correctness depends on the invariant that pdev->tsm is set to NULL on every SPDM session teardown path. The only observed NULL-assignment is in tsm_remove() (line 75), which is the full device-removal cleanup. If an SPDM session can be torn down or become invalid without reaching tsm_remove() — e.g., a TSM backend error, a timeout, or a VMM-initiated session reset that does not propagate through the PCI TSM layer — pdev->tsm would remain non-NULL and connect_show (and transitively authenticated_show) would report a false positive. No session-liveness probe (heartbeat, challenge-response, or SPDM GET_VERSION) is performed before reporting state.
  [RISK] drivers/virt/coco/guest/report.c:262-280  read_cached_report
      read_cached_report implements generation-based freshness tracking using read_generation vs write_generation counters. If outblob is NULL or the generations mismatch, it returns -EWOULDBLOCK to force regeneration. However, write_generation is only incremented by userspace-driven config writes (try_advance_write_generation at lines 109, 139, 163, 189, 205 — all triggered by configfs store operations for inblob, privlevel, service_provider, userdata, manifest_req). There is no mechanism for the TSM backend to asynchronously invalidate the cache when the underlying hardware/device state changes (e.g., a device is hot-unplugged, an SPDM session is renegotiated, or firmware measurements change between generations). If a backend populates outblob once and userspace never writes new config, stale measurements are served indefinitely — directly matching the vulnerability pattern of accepting cached device attestation without fresh cryptographic proof.
  [RISK] drivers/virt/coco/guest/report.c:282-319  tsm_report_read
      tsm_report_read orchestrates the fast-path (cached) vs slow-path (regenerate) decision. The double-check at lines 303-305 re-verifies under the write lock whether another thread already regenerated the report, preventing redundant re-generation. However, this TOCTOU guard only protects against concurrent userspace readers, not against the underlying device state changing between generations. The decision to regenerate is triggered solely by the generation-counter mismatch from read_cached_report. Once a report is cached with matching generations, there is no timestamp, nonce, or freshness token from the TSM backend that would invalidate it due to external device state changes. An attestation verifier reading from this path receives measurements that are 'fresh' only in the sense of having been generated after the last config write, not 'fresh' in the cryptographic sense of reflecting the current live device state.
  [OK] drivers/virt/coco/sev-guest/sev-guest.c:481-580  sev_report_new
      This function generates a fresh SEV-SNP guest attestation report via the get_ext_report() hypercall (line 525). Every invocation issues a new VMGEXIT to the hypervisor to obtain a live attestation report signed by the AMD PSP — it does not cache or return stale measurements. The function operates on VM-level attestation (the guest VM attesting itself), NOT on PCIe device attestation. Grep confirms no SPDM-related return codes (SEV_RET_SPDM_REQUEST, SEV_RET_SPDM_ERROR) exist anywhere in this file. The function is architecturally unrelated to the device-stale-attestation vulnerability pattern described in the spec. It is the backend that populates the outblob consumed by tsm_report_read above, so the freshness concern lies in the caching layer (report.c), not in this backend which always generates fresh measurements.

── opensbi ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #137  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Clarifies that device measurements must be live-fetched (not cached) and that only the complete transcript ending with the signed final MEASUREMENTS is authoritative; trusting stale or partial measurements enables attestation bypass.
  [RISK] lib/utils/mpxy/fdt_mpxy_rpmi_mm.c:14-61  mpxy_rpmi_mm_setup
      Static global 'rsp' (line 14) caches the RPMI_MM_SRV_GET_ATTRIBUTES response once at init via rpmi_normal_request_with_status (lines 41-44). The cached MM attributes (shmem address/size, version) are device identity metadata, never refreshed for the system lifetime. The shmem is then registered into the root domain memrange (line 58) based on this one-time fetch. A device whose firmware was compromised post-boot would serve stale identity data indefinitely. The correctness of the entire MM communication path depends on the unenforced invariant that the device remains uncompromised across the system lifetime.
  [RISK] lib/utils/mpxy/fdt_mpxy_rpmi_mm.c:63-93  mpxy_rpmi_mm_xfer
      For RPMI_MM_SRV_GET_ATTRIBUTES (lines 73-79), returns data reconstructed from the static 'rsp' cache without re-fetching from the device, with no freshness check, nonce, or signing verification. For RPMI_MM_SRV_COMMUNICATE (line 83), passes through to live mbox_chan_xfer() — the path SPDM GET_MEASUREMENTS/MEASUREMENTS transcripts would take — but performs no transcript-chain integrity validation on the opaque payload. A replayed or truncated SPDM transcript would pass through unchecked. The function acts as a transparent proxy without attestation awareness.
  [RISK] lib/utils/mpxy/fdt_mpxy_rpmi_mbox.c:244-346  mpxy_rpmi_mbox_init
