PR #137  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Clarifies that device measurements must be live-fetched (not cached) and that only the complete transcript ending with the signed final MEASUREMENTS is authoritative; trusting stale or partial measurements enables attestation bypass.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_sbi.c:181-220  vmm_sbi_ecall
      SBI dispatch handles only RFNC/TIME/IPI in-kernel; all unknown extensions (including any future CoVE-IO) return false and fall through to userspace handling via VM_EXITCODE_ECALL. There is no interception point for CoVE-IO attestation SBI calls (e.g., sbi_covg_get_device_measurements). If a CoVE-IO handler were added, the current 'default: return false' path provides no kernel-level gate to enforce mandatory re-fetching of the signed SPDM transcript before returning control to the guest. Safe only because no CoVE-IO guest exists yet.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_riscv.c:447-524  riscv_handle_world_switch
      The world-switch trap handler forwards virtual supervisor ecalls (SCAUSE_VIRTUAL_SUPERVISOR_ECALL) to vmm_sbi_ecall() at line 500. On failure (return false), all 8 guest a0-a7 registers are unconditionally copied into vme->u.ecall.args and the exit is set to VM_EXITCODE_ECALL with no inspection of the SBI extension ID or arguments. A CoVE-IO device-attestation SBI call would transit from guest VCPU registers to the host handler with zero kernel-level scrutiny of what attestation operation is being requested or what cached measurements the guest is supplying.
  [RISK] usr.sbin/bhyve/riscv/vmexit.c:309-342  vmexit_ecall
      Userspace SBI dispatch handles SRST, BASE, HSM, PUTCHAR/GETCHAR; returns SBI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED for all other extensions (line 333-334). No CoVE-IO handler exists. If CoVE-IO dispatch were added here, the vulnerability pattern would manifest: a userspace handler that trusts guest-supplied cached device measurements without re-fetching the complete signed SPDM transcript (GET_MEASUREMENTS/MEASUREMENTS pairs) would satisfy the spec's violation condition and allow a compromised device to pass attestation. Currently safe only by absence.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/sbi.h:68-124  (SBI extension ID definitions)
      Defines SBI extension IDs for BASE (0x10), TIME, IPI, RFNC, HSM, SRST, and legacy extensions. No SBI_EXT_ID_COVG or any CoVE-IO extension ID is defined. The namespace boundary where CoVE-IO extension IDs would be defined is empty. Any future implementation must first create these definitions, and the absence means no code can even reference a CoVE-IO SBI extension by symbolic name, making it structurally impossible to implement the spec's requirement of re-fetching the signed transcript through the SBI layer.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/sbi.h:52-52  (SBI_IMPL_ID_COFFER definition)
      Defines SBI_IMPL_ID_COFFER=6, identifying the Coffer confidential-computing SEE—the RISC-V reference firmware that implements CoVE-IO SBI services. The existence of this implementation ID without any corresponding CoVE-IO extension dispatch in vmm_sbi.c or extension ID definitions in sbi.h is a structural gap: the kernel can identify the CoVE SEE (via SBI_BASE_GET_IMPL_ID) but has no code path to use its confidential-computing SBI services. A guest could discover it runs on Coffer and expect CoVE-IO services that don't exist, creating a false sense of security.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm.c:722-726  vm_run
      The VM_EXITCODE_ECALL case in the main vCPU execution loop unconditionally sets retu=true to forward to userspace without any attestation check. A CoVE-aware kernel would need to intercept CoVE-IO ecalls here to verify device measurements against the signed transcript before returning control to the guest. The current code treats all ecall exits identically, providing no differentiation between benign SBI calls (BASE, TIME) and attestation-sensitive CoVE-IO calls that affect the TEE trust boundary.
